#! /usr/bin/env -S less -k
# nb: the line above is a shbang line (for giggles).
# This line and any that don't match this regex are comments:
# /^#(command|line-edit|env)/
# That means that the next comment-like line below actually defines the command section:

#command

# Control-D, which I'm now permanently marked with "end of input"
# should scroll to the end of the file, and then quit less
# (read: goto-eof; then quit)
# Amusingly, this emulates --QUIT-AT-EOF behavior but specifically and ONLY for Ctrl+D,
# even though I generally like the behavior of --quit-at-eof (go to eof 2x to quit) instead
^d goto-end Q

# The traditional quit is insufficient for my needs - I really like ^d's behavior, and want it as this bare quit
q goto-end Q

# A spare 'quit', for safety
^q quit

# Control-G, which runs a status by default. That's weird - I keep expecting it to just be Shift+G, so now it does
# what G does, which is goto-end
^g goto-end

# TODO: Enhancement: what if these "spill the buffers and leave" somehow magically kept the buffer in a /tmp file
# and less was smart enough to open the most recent of them on startup if no other input is provided?

# "Next commit" - typing c gets you to search for the next commit-like label
# by essentially being the / action, but the regex is always baked in
c forw-search \^commit:? [0-9a-zA-Z]+\n
